Binge-Eating Disorder
Mental Health & Substance Use
Binge eating episodes, Lack of control during eating, Absence of compensatory purging

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MHS-010
Reason for Visit: Severe acute abdominal pain, distension, and severe psychological distress following a massive dietary binge.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden, severe epigastric pain, diaphoresis, and marked abdominal distension following the rapid consumption of an exceptionally large volume of food. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to rule out life-threatening acute gastric dilatation, gastric perforation, or acute pancreatitis, which cannot be safely delayed until the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I couldn't stop eating, and now my stomach is so swollen and the pain is unbearable. I feel so disgusted with myself."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 4-hour episode of eating an amount of food that is definitely larger than what most people would eat. The patient describes a profound sense of lack of control over eating during the episode, eating much more rapidly than normal, and eating until feeling uncomfortably full. The binge involved eating large amounts of food when not feeling physically hungry. The patient reports eating alone because of feeling embarrassed by how much they are eating. Currently, the patient is feeling severely disgusted with oneself, depressed, and very guilty. There is marked distress regarding the binge eating. Crucially, there is an absence of regular inappropriate compensatory behaviors like purging, fasting, or excessive exercise.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound, assuming a fetal position due to acute abdominal discomfort.
Social/Safety Concerns: High emotional distress and self-loathing; risk of subsequent depressive decompensation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/92 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 115 Kg
General: Diaphoretic, visibly uncomfortable, clutching abdomen, emotionally tearful and distressed.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals reactive tachycardia. Respiratory exam shows rapid, shallow breathing due to diaphragmatic splinting from distension. Abdominal exam reveals marked generalized distension, hyperactive bowel sounds, and diffuse tympany on percussion. Palpation elicits diffuse discomfort but no localized point tenderness, no guarding, and no rebound tenderness. No rigid abdomen to suggest perforation. Mental state examination confirms binge eating episodes, severe lack of control during eating, and absence of compensatory purging.
Environmental Observations: Dozens of empty fast-food wrappers and large empty snack containers surrounding the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Not currently taking any prescribed medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Binge-Eating Disorder - Status: Exacerbated (Acute gastrointestinal overload)
Differentials:

Gastric Perforation - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of a rigid, board-like abdomen, lack of rebound tenderness, and stable vital signs (no shock).

Acute Pancreatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of radiation of pain to the back, absence of severe nausea/vomiting, and pain being diffuse rather than sharply localized to the epigastrium.

Bulimia Nervosa - Ruled out/unlikely due to the explicit absence of compensatory purging behaviors (vomiting, laxatives) following the binge episode.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe abdominal pain and distension following extreme hyperphagia. Telephone triage accurately flagged the risk of acute gastric dilatation or perforation—rare but life-threatening surgical emergencies associated with massive binge episodes. Urgent after-hours physical examination of the abdomen was critical to differentiate functional distension from a surgical acute abdomen. Clinical findings confirmed severe distension without peritoneal signs, allowing for conservative home management and avoiding an unnecessary emergency department presentation, but this safety could only be established via immediate in-person assessment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Peppermint Oil capsules and Simethicone to aid with severe flatulence and functional distension. Reassurance provided regarding the absence of a surgical emergency. Expected investigation results (outpatient): comprehensive metabolic panel revealing dyslipidemia or impaired fasting glucose.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement "Delay and Distract." When an urge to binge occurs, mandate a 15-minute timer before eating. During this time, engage in a physically incompatible task (e.g., taking a shower or walking around the block). Reward successful delay with non-food praise or points toward a desired purchase. This breaks the automaticity of the binge cycle.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to an eating disorder specialized dietitian and psychologist.
DME/Safety (Optional): Nil.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days for comprehensive metabolic screening and psychological support.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops vomiting of blood, severe worsening pain that becomes localized or sharp, fever, or if the abdomen becomes rigid to the touch.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Abdominal assessment is clinical; severe ongoing pain may require imaging.
Clinical Course: The acute gastrointestinal discomfort will resolve over 12-24 hours as the stomach empties. The psychological distress requires long-term management.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your pain suddenly becomes sharp, if your stomach feels hard like a board, or if you start vomiting blood or dark material, you must call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your stomach is severely stretched but not torn. The medications provided will help break up gas bubbles to relieve some of the pressure.
Actionable Steps: Rest in an upright or semi-reclined position to aid digestion. Do not force yourself to vomit, as this can cause tears in your esophagus. Hydrate with small sips of water.

